facts:
A **straight** segment means a constant speed; a **curved** segment means the speed changes throughout that part.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\begin{scope}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.3);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(3.5,0);
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0.3)--(3.2,2.9);
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.5,1.6) {Distance};
\node[font=\small] at (1.7,-0.55) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.7,-1.25) {Straight: constant speed};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[xshift=6.4cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.3);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(3.5,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:3.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.3+0.254*\x*\x});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.5,1.6) {Distance};
\node[font=\small] at (1.7,-0.55) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.7,-1.25) {Curved: speed changing};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A curve getting **steeper** shows the object **speeding up**; a curve getting **flatter** shows it **slowing down**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\begin{scope}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.3);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(3.5,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:3.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.28*\x*\x});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.5,1.6) {Distance};
\node[font=\small] at (1.7,-0.55) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.7,-1.25) {Getting steeper: speeding up};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[xshift=6.4cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.3);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(3.5,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:3.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-0.28*(\x-3.2)*(\x-3.2)+2.87});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.5,1.6) {Distance};
\node[font=\small] at (1.7,-0.55) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.7,-1.25) {Getting flatter: slowing down};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

